WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.560
 >> Hello, and welcome to Python for Everybodybody.

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:07.200
 I'm Charles Severance, and now we're going to take a look at how we would write some

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.360
 code to do some parsing, read some data.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:15.240
 As a matter of fact, we're going to read through our famous mailbox data, look for

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:19.720
 lines that begin with from space, and extract the third word.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:22.840
 As a matter of fact, we already have some of this code already written.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:23.840
 We're going to debug it.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.460
 We're going to look at code, and we're going to debug it.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:27.460
 So here we go.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:28.460
 Here we have it.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:30.520
 So it's a pretty basic program.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:36.800
 It opens a file, loops through the file, throws away the white space, splits it into words,

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.360
 and checks to see if the zeroth word, the first word, is from, and if it's not, we skip

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.280
 and read the next line.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.400
 And otherwise, if we find a line that starts with from space, then we print the third word,

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:49.400
 which is word_2.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:55.400
 Okay, so this is what we've got, and we've carefully saved this file into the same folder

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:59.000
 that we've got, ex08.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:07.220
 And so let's go ahead, cd desktop python for everybody ex_08.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:10.240
 And so this is some files.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.760
 We got our day of the week, python, and our inbox short, so that's sitting there, okay?

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:17.080
 And so let's run this program.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:24.920
 This is the program that we've got right here, python3dow.py.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:26.880
 And it doesn't work.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.840
 Now, by now you've seen a few tracebacks, and there you go.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:38.600
 So when you look at a traceback, you think to yourself, well, I made a mistake, and you've

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.420
 gotten pretty good at looking at that line.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:42.420
 So there you are.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:43.420
 You're like, this is the line.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:48.240
 There must be something wrong on this line, and you want to change it.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.760
 But that line's not actually the problem in this particular thing.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:54.960
 And so you've got to be careful sometimes, and one of the things that you didn't notice

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.880
 in this one right away is that it actually worked.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.320
 It printed the first line out.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:06.720
 So if we take a look at our data set, it found the line started with from space, it split

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.320
 it and printed out the third word.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.360
 And it blew up later.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.600
 And so part of the problem is that we don't know what it was doing when it blew up.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:23.680
 And so the first thing I like to do in this kind of a situation is find the line and make

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.740
 sure there's a print statement right before it.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:32.760
 And so I'm going to print words colon and then comma WDS.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:37.840
 I want to print right before the line that blows up so that I know really when this finally

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.240
 does blow up what was going on in that line.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.440
 So I'm going to run it again, and do I forget to save it?

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:48.720
 No, I forgot to save it.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:49.720
 Look at that.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:50.720
 See the little blue dot?

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.880
 Forgot to save it.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.960
 So now we see a whole bunch of output, and we see that it's actually doing a whole lot

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:59.960
 of work before it's blowing up.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:04.720
 And so you see that it prints the words out from that first line and prints out Saturday,

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.320
 which is exactly what we expect.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.240
 It's the third word in the line, and then reads a whole bunch of stuff.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:13.240
 And actually, what it's doing now is ignoring-- let me just put something here.

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:21.280
 I'm going to say print ignore.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.000
 So I can keep track of when these lines are being ignored.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.360
 So let's run it again and have the word ignore pop up.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.960
 And so it's doing a lot of ignoring.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:35.480
 It finds these words, prints out Saturday, reads this line and ignores it, reads this

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.640
 line and ignores it, reads this line and ignores it.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:41.760
 So a lot of stuff's going on here that you might not realize.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.720
 And so we have to take a look at what the problem is.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.160
 And so it is now blowing up word sub 0.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.360
 And now we can scroll down, and we can look at exactly what happened right before the

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:53.360
 traceback.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:56.840
 So we really now know exactly what happened before the traceback.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:03.920
 And the interesting thing is that there is an empty string-- I mean, empty array.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.720
 There's an array with zero items.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:09.840
 Now I'm going to print the line out, too.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.000
 Print line colon-- now I haven't changed my program at all.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.800
 I'm just trying to figure out what's going on here.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.840
 So I'll save that, and I'm going to run it.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:22.440
 And we've got a lot of stuff, and it's still working.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:27.400
 It reads a line, it reads a line, splits it into words, and then prints out Saturday,

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.680
 which is the third word on the line.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:34.040
 Now here it reads a line, and this line is a blank line.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:38.520
 And because it's a blank line, the split returns no words.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:39.800
 And that's what blows up.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:43.700
 And the problem now is, oh, wait a sec, list index out of range.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:48.680
 So word sub 0 is not valid, which is the first word, when there are no words.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:52.120
 So this is a statement that works most of the time.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:55.600
 Now you might think, oh, I want to just put a try and accept in there.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.840
 Well, the right thing to do is to say to yourself, oh, wait a second.

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:13.820
 If I don't have enough words, if the length of the words is less than 1, continue.

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:15.840
 So basically, it's going to come through here.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:16.840
 It's going to split it.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:21.720
 And if we don't have any words, meaning it's a blank line, then we're going to skip it.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.920
 So let's run that.

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:26.600
 So now this ran all the way to the end.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:27.600
 It did a lot of stuff.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:33.760
 And it did not blow up specifically, didn't have a traceback.

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:38.320
 Another way to protect this would be to-- I'll take this part out.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:43.840
 This is called a guardian pattern, right?

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:44.840
 Guardian pattern.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:47.240
 Because this is dangerous.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:48.280
 This could blow up.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:52.240
 But it won't blow up if it makes it past here.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.780
 It won't come through there under the conditions that are causing it to blow up.

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:59.720
 Another way to do this might be to protect it as follows.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:02.520
 To say, oh, wait a sec.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:09.920
 If the line is a blank line, no, continue.

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:12.000
 So now what we're going to do is we're going to skip blank lines.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:21.980
 I even say this, print skip blank.

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:25.540
 So if it's a blank, we're going to skip blank and keep going.

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:27.200
 This will skip blank lines.

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:28.600
 It'll come through here.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:30.820
 And this will skip lines that don't have from.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:35.360
 But because we're not processing blank lines, word sub 0 always works.

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:37.320
 So I can run this code.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:38.320
 And it works again.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:40.160
 So here we have a blank line.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:41.160
 We skipped it.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:42.160
 Here we have a blank line.

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:43.160
 We skipped it.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:45.320
 Now here we had a non-blank line, so we parsed it.

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:46.880
 But then we ignored it.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:51.220
 And then up here, we'll find a from somewhere.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:52.680
 Let's find a from.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:07:04.440
 It comes-- oh, no, I just ignore, ignore.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:13.240
 I've got too much debug print.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:14.240
 I can't find it.

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:22.440
 Here, I'll just hunt for from with find.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:24.040
 OK, so there we go.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:24.960
 There it's from.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:26.360
 And we print the thing out.

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:29.880
 So we're getting a lot of extra stuff.

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:32.480
 So I'm going to comment out some of these debugs.

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:37.760
 And I'm actually just going to get rid of this whole skipping

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:38.420
 of the blank line.

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:39.800
 I'm going to do it with the words.

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:42.440
 I'm going to go back to the guardian we had before.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:53.720
 If the number of words that we got, len of words,

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:59.720
 is less than 1, continue.

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:01.820
 OK, so now this is going to be a working program.

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:10.160
 Oops, I've got to take another print statement out.

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:11.940
 Got to take another print statement out.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:13.480
 We sort of know what we're doing here.

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:19.160
 OK, so this looks like a pretty safe thing.

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:22.640
 This guardian is protecting this dangerous.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:24.220
 I'll get rid of that one too.

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:27.240
 This is the word that was our traceback.

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:29.640
 And nothing else in this thing changed from when we started,

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:31.800
 except we've added this little guardian.

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:34.000
 Now the interesting thing is, if it comes through here

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:36.200
 and prints word sub 2, what happens

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:41.240
 if somehow we find a line that has from as its first word,

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:43.840
 and there's only one word on, this is going to blow up.

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:45.840
 So we can make our guardian a little stronger.

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:50.920
 And we can say, you know what, we're

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:53.560
 going to skip this line if it doesn't have three words in it.

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:55.540
 So it has to have at least three words.

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:57.960
 And if we see less than three words, we're going to skip it.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:02.360
 And that just makes the guardian a bit stronger.

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:04.520
 And so the program works safely.

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:06.760
 And you see these things where sometimes you

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:10.000
 want to check to see reasonable, that your assumptions

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.960
 about the data are reasonable and skip things

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:14.960
 where the data is not reasonable.

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:16.720
 So that's one guardian pattern.

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:19.040
 Let me show you a slightly different way to do this.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:21.080
 And this is with an or statement.

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:24.640
 So I'm going to take this code, copy that,

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:27.240
 and put it here with or.

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:29.840
 Get rid of all this stuff.

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:35.200
 This is the guardian in a compound statement.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:43.360
 So what we're saying is if there are less than three words

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:49.020
 on the line or if the first word is not from, continue.

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:52.120
 Now, we're doing this in order because the way it works

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:56.000
 is or is true if either that's true or this is true.

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:59.120
 But if it knows that this is true,

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:00.800
 then it doesn't bother checking this.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:02.920
 And the checking of this is what blows up,

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:04.680
 what causes the traceback.

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:06.880
 So if we flip this order, it would fail.

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:09.400
 If we do it in this order, it will work.

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:13.320
 So let's do this one right.

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:14.440
 It works.

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:22.520
 But if I get this backwards, it's

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:25.560
 going to check this before it checks this.

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:29.720
 And we're going to go back to failing again.

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:32.320
 So you've got to get the order of these things right.

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:39.160
 The guardian comes before in the or.

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:40.800
 The guardian comes before.

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:43.320
 And if this is true, then it doesn't check this.

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:45.680
 This is called short-circuit evaluation,

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:47.800
 where it knows that as long as this part's true,

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:50.800
 it doesn't evaluate this second part.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:54.840
 And so now we have a guardian in a compound statement.

00:10:54.840 --> 00:10:56.640
 You'll see this a lot.

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:59.600
 Sometimes if it's more complex, you do it in multiple statements

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:03.040
 or you fall through, check for sanity, check for sanity,

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:06.500
 and only run the code.

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:10.280
 So I hope that that was useful to you,

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:12.840
 looking a little bit about how to debug where you don't just

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:16.080
 start chopping on the line that had the problem.

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:17.840
 It's not always that line, because we never

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:18.920
 did change that line.

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:20.960
 Although we did change it a little bit at the end.

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:22.200
 We added this guardian here.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:24.640
 But we also fixed it without it.

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:26.240
 Sometimes you add some print statements

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:28.200
 to figure out what's going on before you just

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:30.360
 start chopping on that line.

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:32.920
 So again, I hope this helps.

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:34.480
 Thanks.

